"""
Reads a signal on one clock from another clock: on each tick of the reading clock, `y` is the
value `u` had at the most recent tick of `u`'s own clock, or `init` before that clock has ticked
at all. A source tick simultaneous with the reader's counts, so a reader whose period is a
multiple of the source's sees the value computed in the same instant and the crossing adds no
delay.

This is the rate transition `FurutaMPCMultirate` is built on: the 1 kHz sensing and velocity
estimation feeding the 125 Hz MPC. It is direction-agnostic -- with the reader slower than the
source it sub-samples, faster and it holds -- so the name says what this model uses it for, not
what the block can do.

**Temporary, and it should not be copied.** It does *not* relate the two clocks: both are declared
elsewhere, by two `PeriodicClock`s, and "the slow clock is eight times the fast one" lives in two
period literals that nothing checks. Edit one and forget the other and the model silently changes
meaning. That is exactly why the equivalent upstream blocks were rejected
(JuliaComputing/DiscreteComponents.jl#114): a block-level component cannot express a *derived*
clock, because the kernel cannot query or transform the clock of a connected signal. The accepted
design is JuliaComputing/SynchToolkit.jl#190, `SubSample`/`SuperSample` as compiler operators with
a clock-relation graph that infers rate factors and phase offsets instead of re-declaring them.
Delete this file when that lands.

It has to be an `external component` because Dyad has no rate-transition builtin -- its operators
are `der`, `previous`, `pre`, `initial`, `sample`, `hold` and `time` -- which is the same escape
hatch `PeriodicClock`, `Sampler`, `ZeroOrderHold` and `DiscreteComponents.LastValue` take. That
last one is this block on the deprecated one-argument `SynchToolkit.LastValue`, which now warns at
every compile and cannot be given an initial value.

`init` is a structural parameter rather than a normal one because `SynchToolkit.Latest` places its
initial value on the *reading* clock, and a symbolic parameter has no clock at all; structural
means the Julia body bakes a plain number into the operator term, the only form the operator's own
tests exercise. In this model it is never observed: both clocks start at phase 0 and the slow
period is a power-of-two multiple of the fast one, so every slow tick coincides with a fast one.
"""
external component SubSampler@[input inclk, input outclk]
  "The signal to read, on its own clock"
  u = Dyad.RealInput@[inclk]() {^u}
  "The source's most recent value, on the reading clock"
  y = Dyad.RealOutput@[outclk]() {^y}
  "Value of `y` before the source's clock has ticked; never observed when the two clocks share a tick"
  structural parameter init::Real = 0.0
relations
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"doc": {"behavior": false}},
  "_links": {
    "u": {
      "Dyad": {
        "placement": {
          "diagram": {"iconName": "default", "x1": -50, "y1": 450, "x2": 50, "y2": 550, "rot": 0}
        },
        "tags": []
      }
    },
    "y": {
      "Dyad": {
        "placement": {
          "diagram": {"iconName": "default", "x1": 960, "y1": 450, "x2": 1060, "y2": 550, "rot": 0}
        },
        "tags": []
      }
    }
  }
}
end
